        <h2 id="limits" class="rv">When Won't GEO Help You?</h2>
        <p>GEO won't help much if your buyers don't ask AI tools for recommendations yet, or if your site has nothing worth citing. In those cases, the basics come first.</p>

        <div class="rp-ledger rp-wide rv">
          <div class="rp-ledger-col rp-ledger-yes">
            <p class="rp-label">GEO Is Worth It When</p>
            <ul class="rp-ledger-list">
              <li>Your buyers ask AI tools which product, tool or partner to choose.</li>
              <li>You have real expertise, data or results you can publish.</li>
              <li>Google already crawls and indexes your key pages.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="rp-ledger-col rp-ledger-no">
            <p class="rp-label">Fix These First</p>
            <ul class="rp-ledger-list">
              <li>Google can't crawl or index your key pages.</li>
              <li>Your site says little a buyer couldn't read on a competitor's site.</li>
              <li>You need results by a fixed date. Nobody can promise when an AI tool will start naming you.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>If you're not sure which side you're on, ask two or three AI tools the questions your buyers ask, and note who gets named. That hour tells you more than any keyword report.</p>

        <h2 id="faq" class="rv">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="rp-faq">
          <details class="rp-faq-item">
            <summary class="rp-faq-q">What Is the Difference Between GEO and SEO?</summary>
            <div class="rp-faq-a"><p>SEO is the work of ranking your pages in Google's list of links. GEO, or generative engine optimization, is the work of getting your brand named and cited inside AI answers from ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity and Google's AI Overviews. Most of the groundwork is shared, but the win and the way you measure it are different.</p></div>
          </details>
          <details class="rp-faq-item">
            <summary class="rp-faq-q">What Is GEO in SEO?</summary>
            <div class="rp-faq-a"><p>GEO stands for generative engine optimization. It is the part of search work aimed at AI answers rather than ranked links: making your pages easy for AI tools to find, read and quote. It builds on SEO rather than replacing it.</p></div>
          </details>
          <details class="rp-faq-item">
            <summary class="rp-faq-q">What Is AEO?</summary>
            <div class="rp-faq-a"><p>AEO stands for answer engine optimization. In practice it describes mostly the same work as GEO: making your content the answer an AI tool gives. Different vendors use different names for it, and we use GEO.</p></div>
          </details>
          <details class="rp-faq-item">
            <summary class="rp-faq-q">How Is AEO Different From SEO?</summary>
            <div class="rp-faq-a"><p>SEO aims to rank a page in Google's list of links, while AEO aims to have your content used in the answer itself. Most of the groundwork overlaps, including crawlable pages, clear structure and accurate claims. Google says its own AI features need no special optimization beyond good SEO.</p></div>
          </details>
        </div>

